Cano — byte-oriented modal terminal editor

Usage
  cano [--config init.lua] [file]
  cano -h, --help
  cano -v, --version

  A file that does not exist yet opens as an empty buffer and is created on
  the first save.

Modes
  Normal   motions, editing, undo/redo, explorer, recents, search, commands
  Insert   byte insertion, indentation and brace autoclose
  Visual   character/line selection, jumps, delete, yank, indent, format
  Search   wrapped search and s/old/new replacement
  Command  runtime commands and ! shell execution

Global keys
  Ctrl-Q   quit from any mode (refuses if there are unsaved changes)
  Ctrl-S   save and quit (Normal and Insert modes)
  Ctrl-N   toggle explorer in Normal mode (Escape closes it)
  Ctrl-R   toggle the recent-file list in Normal mode (Escape closes it)
  Ctrl-M   toggle markdown display in Normal mode (so does Enter)
  Ctrl-C   acts as Escape in every mode
  Ctrl-Z   suspend to the shell in Normal mode; fg returns (not on Windows)

Markdown display
  Formats the buffer in place: headings, emphasis, code, links, lists,
  quotes, tables and rules are colored and the syntax punctuation is dimmed.
  Bytes keep their cells, so every motion and edit works unchanged. It starts
  on for .md and related extensions, replaces syntax highlighting while it is
  active, and shows as [MD] in the status line.

Mouse
  On by default; :set-var mouse 0 hands the mouse back to the terminal, whose
  own click-to-select it otherwise takes (most terminals still offer that with
  Shift held). Click moves the cursor, click-and-drag selects in Visual mode,
  the wheel scrolls three lines, and clicking or dragging the scrollbar on
  the right scrolls to that position. A click in the explorer or recent list
  selects an entry -- clicking the selected one opens it, while their
  scrollbar only moves the selection.

Autoformat
  :autoformat (or :Autoformat) tidies the buffer's whitespace, as
  vim-autoformat does when no external formatter is set: re-indent from the
  bracket nesting, retab leading whitespace to match indent, then strip
  trailing spaces. Each step has a switch -- autoformat_autoindent,
  autoformat_retab, autoformat_remove_trailing_spaces -- all on by default;
  :set noautoformat_retab matters for a Makefile. [space] f runs it on the
  whole buffer; = in Visual mode runs it on the selected lines, taking every
  line the selection touches whole.
  Brackets inside strings are
  ignored, the line count never changes, and the whole rewrite is one undo
  step.

Invisible characters
  :set list draws them. The default set names every kind:
  tab:> ,trail:.,eol:<,nbsp:_,space:. in ASCII terms, and arrows and middots
  as shipped; :set listchars? reports it. :set listchars=... replaces the
  whole set, and items left out are then not drawn. Items are tab, trail,
  eol, nbsp and space. A backslash escapes the next character, so a space can
  be a glyph. Nothing moves when list is on -- a tab keeps its width. list is
  off by default; [space] l toggles it.

Options
  Anything spelled as a command can also be run from init.lua with
  cano.command, which is how options without a Boolean slot are configured:
  cano.command([[set listchars=tab:> ,trail:.,eol:$]]). A leading : is
  accepted, commands run in order after the setup table, and a Lua long
  string keeps the backslash that :set needs to hold a space in a value.

  :set name / :set noname turn an option on or off, :set name! toggles,
  :set name=value assigns and :set name? reports. Several may be given at
  once, and vim's names are accepted where they differ from Cano's
  (relativenumber, autoindent, shiftwidth, tabstop, lcs).

Backups
  Each save first copies the version on disk into a .backups directory beside
  the file, named <file>.<YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS> in UTC. The newest 10 per file are
  kept. A file that does not exist yet has nothing to copy. On by default;
  :set-var backup 0 turns it off. A backup that cannot be written is reported
  but does not stop the save. Browse them with the explorer.

Recent files
  Ctrl-R lists the files opened before, most recent first. j/k and the arrows
  move, Enter opens, Escape closes. It shares the pane with the explorer. The
  list is kept beside the configuration file (~/.config/cano/recent by
  default), holds 50 entries, and skips files that no longer exist. Both keys
  ask before leaving an unsaved buffer: y writes then opens, n opens and keeps
  the changes, Escape stays put. [space] n and [space] r do the same.

Jump motions
  s and t are vim-easymotion's find motions. Each asks for one character,
  labels every occurrence on screen and jumps to the label you type. s
  searches both directions; t is forward only and stops one byte before the
  match. Labels use asdghklqwertyuiopzxcvbnmfj; nearest-first, growing to two
  keys when there are more matches than keys. A single match jumps at once,
  and Escape cancels. s also works in Visual mode, where picking a label
  extends the selection to it instead of moving a bare cursor.

Search highlighting
  * searches the word under the cursor whole-word and highlights every match;
  / highlights its matches as a substring search. n repeats it forward and N
  backward, both wrapping. The leader is space: [space] i is *, [space] o is
  :nohl.

Insert-mode mappings
  :imap {keys} {rhs} maps a run of keys typed in Insert mode, for example
  ":imap ;; <Esc>". The right-hand side is a key sequence; <...> spellings
  become their byte. Only keys typed in a row complete a mapping, and one
  fires as soon as its keys are all in.

Search and replace
  :%s/foo/bar/g replaces every foo with bar in the file. The range may be %,
  a line number, . or $, or a from,to pair; without one it is the cursor's
  line. Flags: g every match on a line, c confirm each (y/n/a/q), i ignore
  case. \<foo\> matches whole words. Any punctuation may be the delimiter,
  so :%s#http://#https://#g needs no escaping. Patterns are literal text, not
  regular expressions, and one :s command is one undo step.

File commands
  :w       save
  :q       quit if there are no unsaved changes
  :q!      quit and discard unsaved changes
  :wq      save and quit
  :nohl    stop showing the search highlight (:nohlsearch also works)
  :imap    map a key sequence in Insert mode
  :s       substitute; see Search and replace above

Syntax highlighting
  C, C++, Rust and Python are highlighted out of the box, chosen by the file
  extension (c h / cc cpp cxx c++ hh hpp hxx h++ ipp tpp / rs / py pyi pyw /
  sh bash zsh ksh / vim / lua), or by name for the dotfiles that have no
  extension, such as .vimrc, .bashrc and .zshrc.
  The language selects the scanner as well as the word lists, so // is a
  comment in C but floor division in Python, 'a is a Rust lifetime, and # is
  a directive, an attribute or a comment depending on the language. Any other
  extension needs a <extension>.cyntax palette beside the configuration file,
  which also overrides the built-in colors. :set-var syntax 0 turns it off.

Configuration
  If present, the default file is ~/.config/cano/init.lua. Cano uses
  built-in defaults when that file is absent, and options the file does not
  set keep those defaults. Calling setup(table) is optional; its argument
  must be a table when called. syntax, auto_indent, relative, indent and
  undo_size retain the documented Boolean compatibility behavior. cursorline
  marks the cursor's line by underlining it, the way vim's own terminal
  default for CursorLine does; it is off unless set. setup
  returns exit(code[, message]).
